      "writtenOn": "2020-11-25T15:08:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I found that whenever the necessary information is hidden from the builds system in the source files, there are three possible ways:\n\n0. Allow reading source files by the build system (this requires support of dynamic dependency generation, present in some of the build systems, e.g. Haskell\u0027s Shake, but this makes the build system fundamentally more complex).\n\n1. Add the necessary information to the build system (possibly duplicating it, like we do with the manifests). Very tedious work, and requires additional verification build rules to make sure the duplicated information is coherent.\n\n2. Use an \"indirect\" build rule, which means that the build system will generate a build rule that will get the necessary information from source files (in this case read the manifests) and generate the real build rule, and then immediately run it.\n\nApproach 2 has a limitation that it cannot be used to generate dependencies (there is no way to enforce build order from that late stage of the build), therefore whenever exact dependencies are not known to the build system, it has to use a superset of dependencies and an \"indirect\" build rule to trim it down after reading the information from the source files. For dexpreopt the superset would be all Java libraries, which is too bad, so we cannot use approach 2 instead of `uses_libs`/`optional_uses_libs`, but we do use it to trim down compatibility libraries.\n\nApproach 2 can also be used to generate command-line arguments to various tools and otherwise fix the the build rule without changing its dependencies.",
      "parentUuid": "c91eecbf_506ca70f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 84,
        "startChar": 39,
        "endLine": 85,
        "endChar": 23
      },
